==Galician==
{{was fwotd|gl|2013|March|27}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|gl|uce|urce}}
[[File:Uces en Ortegal.jpg|thumb|''uces'']]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|gl|la|ulex}}, itself from a local {{der|gl|qfa-sub}} language. Compare {{cog|pt|urze}}, {{cog|es|urce}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|gl|/ˈuθ/|/ˈus/|q2=western}}

===Noun===
{{gl-noun|f}}

# {{lb|gl|plant}} [[heather]] {{gloss|especially any of several shrub species in the genus ''{{ll|mul|Erica}}''}}
#* {{quote-text|gl|year=1458|editor=José-Luis Novo Cazón|title=El priorato santiaguista de Vilar de Donas en la Edad Media (1194-1500)|page=413|publisher=Fundación Barrié|location=A Coruña
|passage=''et abedes de leuantar enno dicto lugar huna casa enno dicto lugar, de pedra, cuberta de '''huzes''' et de culmo''
|translation=You should build there a house at that place, made of stone, covered with '''heather''' and thatch}}
#* '''1986''', Constantino García, ''Grilos e ralos, rans albariñas'' in Actas do Congresso internacional de estudos sobre Rosalia de Castro e o seu tempo, volume 3, Universidade de Santiago de Compostela, [http://books.google.com/books?id=CR-NcRphIxgC&pg=PA78&dq=%22uz%22#v=onepage page 78]:
#*: {{quote|gl|Dado que existe ''toxo albar'' e que ''albar'' e mesmo ''albariña'' significa nalgunhas zonas de Galicia '''uz''', carpaza, breixo, penso que cabería tamén a posibilidade de interpreta-las ''rans albariñas'' como rans dos breixos.
|translation=Considering that there is ''toxo albar'' and that ''albar'' and even ''albariña'' mean, in some regions of Galicia, '''heather''', rockrose, heaths, I think we could also interpret ''rans albariñas'' as rockrose frogs.}}
#: {{syn|gl|breixo|queiroa|torgo}}
